  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Inspection periods can move incredibly fast, sometimes as quickly as 15 days from the contract's effective date or even just 5 days before closing. When those repair requests come in, you need a plan! First, review the inspection report carefully to understand the scope of requested repairs. Prioritize anything related to safety, structural integrity, or active issues like leaks or electrical problems. These are usually non-negotiable for buyers and lenders. Next, get prompt estimates from qualified contractors. Don't delay—contractors' schedules fill up quickly, and you'll need to know the cost and timeframe to respond effectively. Be ready to negotiate; sometimes a credit for certain repairs is more feasible than getting everything done before closing. Keep open communication with your agent and the buyer's agent. Most importantly, ensure any agreed-upon repairs are done by licensed professionals and that you keep detailed records, including invoices and permits. This documentation is crucial for showing the buyer that the work was completed correctly. If the requested repairs seem too complex or numerous for the timeline, a professional remodeling and restoration service can help you coordinate and complete the necessary work efficiently."
    }
  ],
